<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Max Planck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Max Karl Ernst Ludwig Planck</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Max Karl Ernst Ludwig Planck was a German theoretical physicist, the originator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">quantum theory</a> and one of the founders of modern physics. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>, he studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-von-helmholtz">Hermann von Helmholtz</a> and Gustav Kirchhoff and in 1889 succeeded Kirchhoff at the Friedrich Wilhelm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a>. On 14 December 1900 he presented a derivation resting on the supposition that electromagnetic energy could be emitted only as a multiple of an elementary unit, the origin of the Planck constant, h. He received the 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics and was twice President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a> Wilhelm Society, renamed the Max Planck Society in 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/max-planck.jpg" alt="Max Planck" width="330" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hugo Erfurth · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Max_Planck_by_Hugo_Erfurth_1938cr_-_restoration1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Planck was born on 23 April 1858 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>, the sixth child of Johann Julius Wilhelm Planck, a professor of law, and his second wife, Emma Patzig. He was baptised Karl Ernst Ludwig Marx Planck but by the age of ten signed himself Max. His great-grandfather and grandfather were theology professors at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>. Among his earliest memories was the march of Prussian and Austrian troops into Kiel during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a> in 1864.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1880 Planck became a Privatdozent at the Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München. Initially ignored by the academic community, he worked on heat theory and found, without realising it, the same thermodynamic formalism as Gibbs. In April 1885 he became associate professor of theoretical physics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a> and in 1897 published his Treatise on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermodynamics">Thermodynamics</a>.</p><p>His initiative merged the local physical societies into the German Physical Society in 1898, and he was its president from 1905 to 1909. He turned to black-body radiation in 1894, though, contrary to many textbooks, the ultraviolet catastrophe was not his motive.</p><p>Planck recognised the importance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a>'s special relativity at once, but at first rejected Einstein's hypothesis of light quanta. At the First Solvay Conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1911, which he and Walther Nernst organised, Einstein convinced him. He rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> interpretation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">quantum mechanics</a> and expected wave mechanics to make quantum theory unnecessary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Planck signed the Manifesto of the 93 intellectuals at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. When the Nazis came to power in 1933 he was 74; he saw Jewish colleagues expelled and hundreds of scientists emigrate, and asked those considering leaving to stay, while helping his nephew Hermann Kranold to emigrate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><p>His first wife, Marie Merck, whom he married in 1887, died in 1909, and he married Marga von Hoesslin in 1911. Planck was a Lutheran who held that religion and natural science &quot;do not exclude each other&quot;. He played piano, organ and cello and hosted musical evenings at which Einstein played violin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Max Planck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Research is a pillar of the economy, with the <b>Max Planck</b> Society, the Helmholtz Association and the Fraunhofer Society, and the dual apprenticeship system is copied around the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Before the German Physical Society in Berlin, <b>Max Planck</b> proposed that energy is emitted in discrete packets, or quanta.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-14">14 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
